<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lat Dior</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1842">1842</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lat Dior Diop, Lat Joor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lat Dior Diop was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damel">Damel</a>, or king, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, ruling at intervals between 1862 and 1886. He became a Muslim and resisted French expansion, above all the railway from Dakar to Saint-Louis that crossed his territory. Deposed by the French, he was killed in battle at Dekhelé in 1886 and is honoured as a national hero in Senegal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lat-dior.jpg" alt="Lat Dior" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BelAirMurals6.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lat Dior was the son of Sakhéwère Sokhna Mbaye and the royal Linguère Ngoné Latyr Fall, and belonged through his mother to the Geej or Guedj matriclan, which had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a> many of their rulers. Eligible through that lineage, he was the first damel whose father was not of the Fall family. What is known of his youth comes mostly from the oral histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>: he was born at Keur Amadou Yalla in north-eastern Cayor, studied at a Quranic school, and was already active in politics at 17.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Majojo, the damel installed by the French in 1861, was defeated in battle by his opponents the next year, and they put the young Lat Dior in his place. After a victory at Ngolgol in December 1863 he was beaten at Loro in January 1864 and fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Louis Faidherbe</a> annexed. He joined the marabout Maba Diakhou Bâ of Saloum, became his disciple, and led his armies in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a> and Djolof in 1865; the marabout forces drove back Governor Émile Pinet-Laprade at Pathé Badiane but were defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> at Fandane-Thiouthioune on 18 July 1867.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a>, he defeated the French at Mekhe on 8 July 1869, and on 15 July 1870 they recognised him as damel, confirmed by a treaty early in 1871. With French help, above all heavy artillery, he defeated his former ally Shaikh Amadou Ba at Samba Sadio in February 1875. The alliance broke down in 1883 over the railway, and the governor installed his nephew Samba Lawbe Fall in his place; for three years Lat Dior fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war with a small band.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Samba Lawbe Fall was killed in October 1886, Lat Dior was manoeuvred into battle at Dekhele in late December and killed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> ceased to exist as an independent, united state. His promotion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after his initiation by Maba moved many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> away from an older syncretic Islam towards the faith of the clerics. A large statue of his horse, Maalaw, stands near the great mosque of Dakar, and the Senegalese Army's main camp in Dakar and a stadium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thies">Thiès</a> bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lat Jor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Damel <b>Lat Dior Diop</b> opposed the Dakar to Saint-Louis railway and was killed at Dekhle in 1886, after which France absorbed the kingdom into French Senegal.</blockquote><a href="/state/cayor">Cayor</a></li></ul></section>
